title: claim_timeout 0 (and lock_timeout 0) is accepted as 'never' by config set but
  read back as the 1h default
updated: 2026-10-07T15:53:33Z
---

`karr config set claim_timeout 0` is accepted: `Config->parse_duration` returns 0 for a bare zero (`lib/App/karr/Config.pm:310`, `return 0 if $str =~ /\A0+\z/`), the same value `0s` gives, which k232 defined as 'a claim never expires'. The reader disagrees. `Role::ClaimTimeout::_parse_timeout` (`lib/App/karr/Role/ClaimTimeout.pm:95-102`) starts with

```perl
return $fallback unless $timeout_str;
```

and the string `"0"` is false in Perl, so the fallback (3600 s) is used. `0s` and even `00` are true strings and give 0. A board whose owner switched expiry off with `0` therefore still lets `pick` and `check_claim` take over a claim after one hour -- two agents on one card, with only the 'overriding the expired claim' warning as a trace. A hand-written `claim_timeout: 0` in the config (a YAML integer) behaves the same. `lock_timeout` goes through the same sub (`Cmd/Pick.pm:265`, `Cmd/Unlock.pm:106`): `0` gives the default lock TTL instead of the documented 'disable'.

**Reproduce**

```
karr config set claim_timeout 0      # 'Set claim_timeout = 0'
perl -Ilib -e 'package T; use Moo; sub store{} sub json{} sub quiet{} with "App::karr::Role::ClaimTimeout";
  package main; my $t = T->new; print join(" ", map { $t->_parse_timeout($_) } "0", "0s", "00"), "\n"'
# 3600 0 0
```

**Done when** `_parse_timeout` tests `defined && length` instead of truth, so `0`, `0s` and `00` all mean 0 for both timeouts, and a test beside the k232 one pins `claim_timeout: 0` (string and YAML integer) as 'never expires'.

